  y: { min: -3.5, max: 1.5, label: 'free energy (a.u.)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const hierarchyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Primary — the sequence', note: 'A linear chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. The order is specified by a gene, and it contains every instruction the protein needs.' },
    { math: '2 · Secondary — local patterns', note: 'Segments of the chain fold into repeating shapes: alpha helices and beta sheets, held by hydrogen bonds along the backbone.' },
    { math: '3 · Tertiary — the whole fold', note: 'The entire chain packs into one 3D shape. Hydrophobic side chains bury inside; charged ones face the water. This is the working shape.' },
    { math: '4 · Quaternary — the assembly', note: 'Some proteins are teams: haemoglobin is four chains acting as one. Function can live at this level, not in any single chain.' },
    { math: '5 · Function', note: 'The fold creates the chemistry: a pocket of exactly the right geometry and chemical groups to bind a substrate and transform it.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biochemistry/foundational/protein-structure-and-function">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A protein is a sentence written in a twenty-letter alphabet that folds itself into a machine.
    That single fact organises all of biochemistry: the <strong>sequence</strong> of amino acids
    encodes the <strong>structure</strong>, and the structure performs the <strong>function</strong> —
    catalysis, transport, signalling, structure. Change the sequence and, depending on where you
    change it, you get nothing at all, a subtle shift, or a disease like sickle-cell anaemia.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stakes of the sequence→structure→function chain are best seen in one number: your body
    contains tens of thousands of different proteins, built from the same twenty amino-acid
    building blocks, and every difference between haemoglobin and a digestive enzyme, between
    an antibody and a hair, is the order those twenty are strung in and the shape that order
    folds into. DNA gets the fame, but DNA is the archive; the proteins are the workforce —
    they catalyse the reactions, haul the cargo, pass the signals, and form the walls.
    Understanding how a one-dimensional list becomes a three-dimensional worker is this
    chapter's single task.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start at the beginning of the chain: what the letters are, and how they join.
  </p>

  <h2>Amino acids and the peptide bond</h2>
  <p>
    Every amino acid shares the same core: a central (alpha) carbon bonded to a hydrogen, an
    amino group (−NH₂), a carboxyl group (−COOH), and one variable side chain — the
    <strong>R group</strong> — that is the letter's identity. Glycine's R is a single
    hydrogen; leucine's is a branched oily tail; aspartate's ends in a negative charge;
    lysine's in a positive one; cysteine's ends in a sulfur-bearing thiol that can link to
    another cysteine. The twenty standard amino acids are therefore a chemistry set sorted
    into families: <strong>hydrophobic</strong> (oily — alanine, valine, leucine,
    phenylalanine…), <strong>polar uncharged</strong> (serine, threonine, asparagine…),
    <strong>positively charged</strong> (lysine, arginine, histidine), <strong>negatively
    charged</strong> (aspartate, glutamate), and a few specials (glycine, proline, cysteine).
    At physiological pH the ends are ionised (−NH₃⁺ and −COO⁻) — amino acids are
    <em>zwitterions</em> — which is why they dissolve in water and why proteins carry
    overall charge that shifts with pH.
  </p>
  <p>
    The join is the <strong>peptide bond</strong>: the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid
    links to the amino nitrogen of the next, releasing water — a condensation reaction —
    and the chain so built has direction: an unjoined N-terminus at one end, a C-terminus at
    the other, and sequences are always written N→C, in the order the ribosome made them.
    Two crucial properties follow. The peptide bond is planar and stiff (partial
    double-bond character), so the chain's flexibility lives in the bonds <em>beside</em>
    it — a constraint that narrows the shapes a fold can take. And the backbone is
    polar and repeats every residue: −NH–CH(R)–CO− over and over — which is exactly what
    makes the hydrogen-bond patterns of the next section possible. A chain of two amino
    acids is a dipeptide, a few make an oligopeptide, and dozens to thousands make a
    protein; insulin, a small protein, is 51 amino acids long, titin some 34,000.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the twenty amino acids are not arbitrary and they are not
  used in equal amounts — they are the subset life's chemistry settled on (with glycine and
  alanine found in meteorites, suggesting deep chemical roots), and each organism's proteins
  have characteristic compositions. Nor does "amino acid" mean "nutrient only": of the
  twenty, nine are <em>essential</em> in the human diet (we cannot synthesise them); the
  other eleven we build ourselves from simpler precursors.</p>

  <h2>The four levels of structure</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the four levels of structure, and where function appears" widget="equationStep" config={hierarchyCfg} />
  <p>
    Each level deserves its mechanism spelled out. <strong>Secondary structure</strong> is
    made by the backbone hydrogen-bonding with itself: in the <em>alpha helix</em>, each
    C=O hydrogen-bonds to the N–H four residues along, winding the chain into a spring
    (the keratin of hair is largely helices — which is why a wet hair stretches: the
    hydrogen bonds slip and re-form); in the <em>beta sheet</em>, extended strands lie
    alongside each other, hydrogen-bonding across (silk is largely sheets — strong along
    the strands). Short recognisable combinations of these elements — helix-turn-helix, the
    beta-hairpin — are <em>motifs</em>; larger independently folding units with a job
    (a barrel, a binding pocket) are <em>domains</em>, and many large proteins are beads
    on a string of domains.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tertiary structure</strong> is the whole-chain fold, and its organising rule is
    simple to state: in water, oily things cluster. The hydrophobic side chains pack into a
    greasy interior from which water is largely excluded; polar and charged side chains
    face out, and where a charged one must hide, it hides paired (a <em>salt bridge</em>).
    Cysteine pairs can add covalent cross-links (<em>disulfide bonds</em>) that lock the
    fold — the reason perm solution (which breaks disulfides) reshapes hair. The result of
    all this packing is a molecule that is not a floppy bag but a precise object, with
    grooves, hinges and pockets at particular positions. <strong>Quaternary structure</strong>
    is the assembly of separate chains into one functional unit: <em>haemoglobin</em> is
    four chains (two alpha, two beta) each cradling an iron-bearing haem group; the four
    subunits <em>cooperate</em> — the first oxygen binding nudges the whole assembly,
    making the next three bind more easily, which is why haemoglobin loads up fully in the
    lungs and unloads efficiently in tissue. Function can live at this level and nowhere
    else: no single haemoglobin chain shows cooperativity, because the geometry between
    chains <em>is</em> the mechanism.
  </p>

  <h2>Why proteins fold at all</h2>
  <p>
    A chain of hundreds of amino acids could, in principle, wiggle into an astronomical number of
    shapes — yet it folds to essentially one native structure in milliseconds. The driving force is
    thermodynamic: the cell's water pushes oily (hydrophobic) side chains together so they can hide
    from it, while hydrogen bonds and salt bridges fine-tune the packing. The result is a funnel
    shaped energy landscape: many unfolded starting points, one low-energy native state.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a schematic folding funnel — two energy minima, one native" widget="plotter" config={foldCfg} />

  <p>
    The funnel picture resolves a famous paradox. Cyrus Levinthal noted in 1969 that if a
    100-residue chain sampled shapes randomly — even at one shape per picosecond — it would
    take longer than the age of the universe to find its fold;
    yet small proteins fold in milliseconds. The resolution: folding is <em>not</em> a
    random search. The chain slides down an energy funnel, guided at every step by local
    preferences (helices nucleate, hydrophobics cluster), so it explores only a tiny funnel
    of the possible shapes. Christian Anfinsen's Nobel-winning experiment (ribonuclease,
    1950s–60s) proved the deeper point: a denatured enzyme, freed of its disrupter, refolded
    into a fully active form — the sequence <em>alone</em>, in the right environment,
    specifies the native structure. That result is the theoretical licence for the whole
    field of structure prediction: the information is in the chain.
  </p>

  <h2>Structure is not an ornament</h2>
  <p>
    Enzymes work because the fold places a handful of chemical groups at precisely the right
    distances and angles to stabilise a reaction's transition state — the unstable
    halfway arrangement every reaction must pass through; lowering that barrier is what
    "catalysis" means, and only a folded, rigid-yet-tunable pocket can do it. Haemoglobin carries oxygen
    because four subunits arranged in a particular geometry switch shape when the first oxygen
    binds. Antibodies recognise invaders because their folded loops present a binding surface of
    almost arbitrary shape — six loops of the immunoglobulin domain, varied by gene shuffling,
    give the immune system a surface for almost any molecule. In each case the function <em>is</em>
    the structure. A worked example in miniature: sickle-cell anaemia is caused by a single
    amino-acid substitution — position 6 of the beta chain, glutamate (charged, outward-facing)
    replaced by valine (oily) — which puts a hydrophobic patch on the protein's surface;
    haemoglobins stick to each other there, polymerise into fibres, and distort the red cell.
    One letter out of 146: the entire disease. It also explains why the mutation survives in
    malarial regions — one copy of the gene protects against malaria, the classic case of
    selection balancing harm against benefit.
  </p>

  <h2>When folding fails: chaperones and misfolding disease</h2>
  <p>
    Because function depends on the fold, misfolding is catastrophic. Chaperone proteins exist
    mostly to give new chains a fair chance to reach the native state — and the cell needs
    them more than the funnel picture suggests. The cytoplasm is a <em>crowded</em> place:
    20–40% of its volume is taken by other macromolecules, so a nascent chain emerging from
    the ribosome is surrounded by surfaces it could wrongly stick to, and hydrophobic
    patches exposed mid-fold are danger zones. Chaperones (the Hsp70 and chaperonin
    families) bind exposed hydrophobic regions, shield them, and either hold the chain in a
    folding-competent state or enclose it in a barrel-shaped cavity that lets it fold
    unpolluted. Chaperones are also <em>heat-shock proteins</em> — their production surges
    when temperature rises, because heat shakes proteins loose of their folds. Some
    chaperones even use ATP actively to pull misfolded chains out of traps and give them
    another chance; others escort hopelessly damaged proteins to destruction (the
    proteasome). Folding, in other words, is a managed industrial process, not an
    unavoidable consequence of chemistry alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a misfolded protein escapes that safety net and aggregates, the result is a family of
    diseases. Many misfolded states expose hydrophobic surfaces that stack into highly
    ordered fibres called <em>amyloid</em>: Alzheimer's disease is associated with amyloid
    plaques of the Aβ peptide and tau tangles; Parkinson's with alpha-synuclein aggregates;
    type 2 diabetes with amylin deposits. The strangest case is the <em>prion</em>: a
    misfolded form of a normal brain protein (PrP) that <em>induces</em> correctly folded
    copies to flip into the misfolded shape — an infection by shape alone, with no genome,
    which explains the spongiform encephalopathies (scrapie, BSE/"mad cow", variant CJD)
    and their terrifying resistance to normal sterilisation. Understanding the
    sequence-to-structure map, as structural prediction now does with remarkable accuracy, is
    therefore one of the most consequential problems in biology.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three frontiers sharpen the chapter's ideas. First, the <strong>protein-folding problem's
    computational conquest</strong>: predicting a structure from sequence was biology's
    grand-challenge for fifty years (the CASP blind trials ran from 1994), until DeepMind's
    AlphaFold2 (2020) achieved near-experimental accuracy for most single-domain proteins by
    training on the accumulated structures of the PDB; its predictions now cover hundreds of
    millions of sequences (AlphaFold DB), turning structure into a lookup rather than a
    decade of crystallography — with the honest caveats that it predicts the folded
    <em>state</em>, not the folding <em>pathway</em>, and struggles with the sizable fraction
    of proteins that are intrinsically disordered, existing as ensembles rather than one
    fold. Second, <strong>disorder is a feature</strong>: perhaps a third of eukaryotic
    proteins contain long unstructured regions, which function precisely by being floppy —
    flexible tethers, low-affinity hubs that bind many partners briefly. The textbook
    picture (one chain, one fold, one function) is the skeleton; the modern picture adds
    fuzzy mobile parts. Third, <strong>fold-switching and evolution</strong>: some proteins
    change shape on binding (membrane channels that open), and evolution evidently explores
    structure space by recombining domains and walking through sequences that tolerate
    multiple folds — which reframes "misfolding" as one end of a continuum that biology
    exploits. The one-line summary survives every refinement, though: sequence encodes
    structure (or structural behaviour), and structure performs function — the central
    dogma's working end.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT OpenCourseWare 5.07SC Biological Chemistry I; Berg, Tymoczko, Gatto &amp; Stryer, <em>Biochemistry</em>; Alberts et al., <em>Molecular Biology of the Cell</em>; Anfinsen, Science (1973); Levinthal's paradox discussion (1969); Jumper et al., "Highly accurate protein structure prediction with AlphaFold", Nature (2021).</p>
</TopicPage>
